{"duration_api_ms":163347,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"6dce696f-d939-4124-9907-613827c75adc","total_cost_usd":0.2250556,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11096,"cache_read_input_tokens":4538,"output_tokens":17976,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":14292},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11096,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":17976,"cache_read_input_tokens":4538,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11096,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11096},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":17976,"cacheReadInputTokens":4538,"cacheCreationInputTokens":11096,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.2250556,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":14292,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"merged_document\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\\n\\nAlthough Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the three planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years. In fact, its encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible. The Uranus encounter's geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 1 had only 6.4 days of close study during its flyby.\\n\\nThe first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2's short-range observations of the planet began Jan. 31, 1986, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth. Light conditions were five-hundred times less than terrestrial conditions. Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 ED Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 81,500 kilometers (50,600 miles).\\n\\nDuring its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1852), three new rings in addition to the \\\"older\\\" eight rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center.\\n\\nThe spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus' atmosphere as high as 450 km/h (450.000 meters per hour) and found evidence of a boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers) below the top cloud surface. Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\\n\\nVoyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Ariel, Umbriel, Titania, and Oberon, five of Uranus' smaller moons. In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels. Images of the moon showed a strange object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features that seemed to have no rhyme or reason. Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\\n\\nThe spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was overshadowed four days later by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"slot\": \"title\",\n      \"candidates\": [\n        { \"text\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\", \"document\": \"source_a.md\" },\n        { \"text\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\", \"document\": \"source_b.md\" }\n      ],\n      \"chosen\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\n      \"reason\": \"Both documents share the identical title; base title is kept.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"dispositions\": [\n    { \"segment\": \"a2\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the three planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years.\", \"reason\": \"Corrected nonexistent 'Voyager 3' to 'Voyager 2'\" },\n    { \"segment\": \"a4\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"The Uranus encounter's geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 1 had only 6.4 days of close study during its flyby.\", \"reason\": \"Corrected 'Saturn' to 'Neptune' for chronological sense\" },\n    { \"segment\": \"a5\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"The first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2's short-range observations of the planet began Jan. 31, 1986, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth.\", \"reason\": \"Corrected spacecraft name and encounter year\" },\n    { \"segment\": \"a7\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 ED Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 81,500 kilometers (50,600 miles).\", \"reason\": \"Corrected year and swapped mismatched km/mile figures\" },\n    { \"segment\": \"b1\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\", \"reason\": \"Identical title kept once, from the base document\" },\n    { \"segment\": \"b2\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1852), three new rings in addition to the \\\"older\\\" eight rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center.\", \"reason\": \"Corrected moon count, names, allusion, and naming-tradition year\" },\n    { \"segment\": \"b3\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus' atmosphere as high as 450 km/h (450.000 meters per hour) and found evidence of a boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers) below the top cloud surface.\", \"reason\": \"Corrected unit-conversion arithmetic error\" },\n    { \"segment\": \"b5\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Ariel, Umbriel, Titania, and Oberon, five of Uranus' smaller moons.\", \"reason\": \"Corrected misspelled/misattributed moon names\" },\n    { \"segment\": \"b6\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels.\", \"reason\": \"Corrected 'century-long' to match actual mission timespan\" },\n    { \"segment\": \"b9\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was overshadowed four days later by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\", \"reason\": \"Corrected Challenger date, death toll, and timing relative to flyby\" }\n  ],\n  \"mismatch\": \"\",\n  \"additions\": [\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus\",\n      \"corrects\": \"Voyager 3 had fulfilled its primary mission goals\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Only two Voyager probes were ever launched; no Voyager 3 exists\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"The Uranus encounter's geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune\",\n      \"corrects\": \"possibility of a future encounter with Saturn\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Saturn preceded Uranus in Voyager 2's itinerary; Neptune came next\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Voyager 2's short-range observations of the planet began Jan. 31, 1986\",\n      \"corrects\": \"Voyager 1's short-range observations of the planet began Jan. 31, 1987\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Only Voyager 2 visited Uranus, in 1986, not 1987\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 ED Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 81,500 kilometers (50,600 miles)\",\n      \"corrects\": \"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 ED Jan. 24, 1968, at a range of about 50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles)\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Voyager 2 launched in 1977, so 1968 is impossible; km/mi figures were swapped\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1852)\",\n      \"corrects\": \"discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelina, Ophelia, and Bianca II – obvious allusions to Goethe, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1687)\",\n      \"basis\": \"citation\",\n      \"source\": \"IAU Working Group for Planetary System Nomenclature, Uranian satellite naming convention\",\n      \"reason\": \"Uranus's moons are named for Shakespearean/Popean characters, tradition began 1852\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"wind speeds in Uranus' atmosphere as high as 450 km/h (450.000 meters per hour)\",\n      \"corrects\": \"450 km/h (72400 meters per hour)\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"450 kilometers equals 450,000 meters, not 72,400\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"photos of Miranda, Ariel, Umbriel, Titania, and Oberon, five of Uranus' smaller moons\",\n      \"corrects\": \"photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariele, Umbrella, and Titan, five of Uranus' smaller moons\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Titan is a moon of Saturn; Uranus's major moons are named differently\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels\",\n      \"corrects\": \"nearly century-long travels\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Voyager 2 launched in 1977, about nine years before the Uranus flyby\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"overshadowed four days later by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986\",\n      \"corrects\": \"interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch Feb. 28, 1986\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Challenger disaster killed seven crew on Jan. 28, 1986, four days after the flyby\"\n    }\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":140776,"type":"result","duration_ms":163418,"uuid":"0e0e4766-76cd-4ba5-992f-c1b10c21713d","ttft_stream_ms":1405,"time_to_request_ms":68,"first_content_frame_ms":1405,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
